#![allow(clippy::excessive_precision)]
//! Golden-parity test for cartalith-engine::generate_terrain's full
//! carveRiverValleys tail (reference HTML lines 8761-8789), extending the
//! tectonic-substrate-through-flow(area) fixture in golden_parity_pipeline.rs
//! through the entire default generate() pipeline: light stream-power pass,
//! isostatic rebound, river-network topology, vector channel carve +
//! riverMask/riverFloor lock, then a final flow(discharge) + refreshClimate()
//! against the carved field (carveRiverValleys's own step 3, reference HTML
//! line 8784: `computeFlow(true); refreshClimate();`).
//!
//! **Regenerated 2026-08-15 with `WorldParams::defaults`'s real values**
//! (`volc.provinces`/`terrain_wind_deflection`/`currents` all `true`,
//! matching JS -- cartalith-native/docs/CHANGELOG.md) -- the previous
//! version of this fixture pinned all three off, predating those flips.
//! No overrides needed in the test body anymore; this is what a plain
//! `WorldParams::defaults` run actually produces now. Captured by letting
//! the real reference `generate()` run to completion under Node (no
//! monkey-patch/early-abort needed this time -- carving is the last stage
//! that touches any field this test checks) and reading `field`/
//! `tempField`/`rainField`/`flowField`/`riverMask` directly afterward.
//!
//! w_iters lowered to 12 (from the state default 70) purely to keep this
//! fixture's generation script fast; the weather sim's own golden tests
//! already cover its behavior at other iteration counts.
//!
//! **Tolerance, not exact equality** -- unlike `golden_parity_pipeline.rs`
//! (the pre-carve segment, which stays bit-identical). This pipeline chains
//! dozens of independent `Math.hypot` call sites across many stages
//! (`computeStress`'s boundary normal, `streamPowerKernel`'s D8 distances,
//! `enforceChannelDescent`'s per-pixel radius, `buildWind`'s wind-vector
//! magnitude, `simulateWeather`'s advection distance) -- each one a
//! potential source of the same 1-ULP JS/Rust divergence
//! `golden_parity_weather.rs` already documents in detail (`Math.hypot` is
//! ECMA-262 "implementation-approximated," not required to be
//! correctly-rounded like `+`/`-`/`*`/`/`). A pipeline this long
//! accumulates that noise from multiple sites rather than one, so bit-exact
//! equality isn't the right bar here -- `river_mask` stays exact, though:
//! it's a discrete decision (`target < fld[i]`), and both cases matched it
//! exactly even with the underlying floats off by ~1e-7, which is itself
//! evidence the divergence is float noise, not a wrong carve decision.
fn assert_close(actual: &[f32], expected: &[f32], label: &str) {
    const ATOL: f32 = 1e-4;
    const RTOL: f32 = 1e-4;
    for (i, (&a, &e)) in actual.iter().zip(expected.iter()).enumerate() {
        let tol = ATOL + RTOL * e.abs();
        assert!(
            (a - e).abs() <= tol,
            "{label} index {i}: got {a}, expected {e} (diff {}, tol {tol})",
            (a - e).abs()
        );
    }
}

